 #30363d\\",maxHeight:\\"300px\\",overflowY:\\"auto\\",margin:0,whiteSpace:\\"pre-wrap\\",wordBreak:\\"break-word\\"},children:v.preview}),v.preview.length>=500&&f.jsx(\\"div\\",{style:{fontSize:\\"9px\\",color:\\"#6b7280\\",marginTop:\\"4px\\",fontStyle:\\"italic\\"},children:\\"... preview truncated at 500 characters\\"})]}),f.jsx(\\"div\\",{style:{marginTop:\\"8px\\",paddingTop:\\"8px\\",borderTop:\\"1px solid #3a3f52\\"},children:f.jsx(\\"div\\",{style:{fontSize:\\"9px\\",color:\\"#6b7280\\",fontFamily:\\"monospace\\"},children:v.file_path})})]})})})]},`${v.file_path}-${g}`)})})},Eg=[{title:\\"Origins of Ska\\",fact:\\"Ska originated in Jamaica in the late 1950s, predating reggae and rocksteady. The name \'ska\' might come from the scratchy guitar sound or from the greeting \'Skavoovie!\'\\"},{title:\\"Three Waves of Ska\\",fact:\\"Ska has gone through three waves: Jamaican ska (1960s), 2-Tone (late 1970s UK), and third wave (1990s US). Now your AI agents are part of the fourth wave!\\"},{title:\\"The Skank Dance\\",fact:\\"The \'skank\' is the signature ska dance move - running in place while pumping your arms. It\'s all about the offbeat upstroke energy!\\"},{title:\\"2-Tone Records\\",fact:\\"The British 2-Tone movement in the late 70s combined ska with punk energy. Bands like The Specials and Madness pioneered this checkerboard-loving scene.\\"},{title:\\"Horn Section Power\\",fact:\\"A proper ska band needs a horn section! Trumpet, trombone, and saxophone create that unmistakable punchy brass sound.\\"},{title:\\"The Offbeat\\",fact:\\"Ska is defined by its offbeat rhythm - the guitar \'chank\' or upstroke on the 2nd and 4th beats. It\'s the opposite of rock\'s downbeat emphasis.\\"},{title:\\"Rude Boys\\",fact:\\"In Jamaica, \'rude boys\' were young rebels who loved ska. The term became synonymous with ska culture and fashion.\\"},{title:\\"Checkered Pattern\\",fact:\\"The black and white checkered pattern symbolizes ska\'s message of racial harmony, particularly during the 2-Tone movement.\\"},{title:\\"Prince Buster\\",fact:\\"Prince Buster is one of ska\'s founding fathers. His song \'Al Capone\' directly inspired Madness\'s name and sound.\\"},{title:\\"Ska-Punk Fusion\\",fact:\\"Third wave ska fused with punk rock, creating bands like Operation Ivy, Rancid, and Sublime. The DIY attitude fit perfectly!\\"},{title:\\"Studio One\\",fact:\\"Studio One in Kingston, Jamaica was the birthplace of recorded ska. Producer Coxsone Dodd helped define the genre\'s sound.\\"},{title:\\"Walk Don\'t Run\\",fact:\\"The original ska bands often covered American R&B songs, speeding them up and adding that distinctive offbeat guitar.\\"},{title:\\"The Specials\\",fact:\\"The Specials were the flagship band of 2-Tone. Their song \'Ghost Town\' became an anthem during 1981\'s UK riots.\\"},{title:\\"Madness\\",fact:\\"Madness had more UK chart hits in the 1980s than any other band. They started as a pure ska band before evolving their sound.\\"},{title:\\"Selecter\\",fact:\\"The Selecter, featuring Pauline Black, was one of the few 2-Tone bands with a female lead singer. They promoted gender equality in ska.\\"},{title:\\"Boss Reggae\\",fact:\\"\'Boss reggae\' was an early term for ska, used before the genre had its own name. It reflected the genre\'s aspirational attitude.\\"},{title:\\"Sound Systems\\",fact:\\"Jamaican sound systems - mobile disco units - were crucial in spreading ska. DJs would compete with the loudest and best tracks.\\"},{title:\\"Rudy Message\\",fact:\\"Many ska songs contain social commentary. \'A Message to You, Rudy\' by The Specials tells young \'rude boys\' to stop fighting.\\"},{title:\\"Toasting\\",fact:\\"Ska helped develop \'toasting\' - talking rhythmically over music - which later influenced rap and hip-hop.\\"},{title:\\"Ska Fashion\\",fact:\\"2-Tone ska fashion included pork pie hats, thin ties, loafers, and sharp suits - influenced by mod and Jamaican rude boy style.\\"},{title:\\"Desmond Dekker\\",fact:\\"Desmond Dekker\'s \'Israelites\' was the first reggae/ska song to hit #1 in the UK charts in 1969.\\"},{title:\\"Mighty Mighty Bosstones\\",fact:\\"The Mighty Mighty Bosstones helped pioneer ska-core in the 90s. Their song \'The Impression That I Get\' was a radio staple.\\"},{title:\\"Less Than Jake\\",fact:\\"Less Than Jake combined ska with pop-punk, proving the genre could evolve while keeping its horn-driven heart.\\"},{title:\\"Rocksteady\\",fact:\\"Rocksteady developed from ska in 1966 Jamaica, slowing down the tempo. It was the bridge between ska and reggae.\\"},{title:\\"Catch This Beat\\",fact:\\"The upstroke guitar pattern in ska is sometimes called \'catch this beat\' - it\'s the rhythmic hook that defines the genre.\\"},{title:\\"Ska In Film\\",fact:\\"Ska has appeared in many films! \'Rude Boy\' (1980) documented The Clash\'s ska influences during the 2-Tone era.\\"},{title:\\"Trojan Records\\",fact:\\"Trojan Records brought Jamaican ska and reggae to the UK market, becoming a crucial bridge between cultures.\\"},{title:\\"Skatalites\\",fact:\\"The Skatalites were Jamaica\'s premier ska band, backing most of the genre\'s hits. Members later formed several legendary reggae bands.\\"},{title:\\"One Step Beyond\\",fact:\\"Madness\'s \'One Step Beyond\' was actually a cover of a Prince Buster song - showing the direct lineage from Jamaican ska to 2-Tone.\\"},{title:\\"Ska Orchestra\\",fact:\\"Some modern ska bands feature full orchestras! The genre has evolved to include jazz, orchestral, and electronic influences.\\"},{title:\\"Offbeat Origins\\",fact:\\"The ska offbeat rhythm was influenced by American R&B radio stations that Jamaicans could pick up on their radios.\\"},{title:\\"Night Nurse\\",fact:\\"Gregory Isaacs\' \'Night Nurse\' blended ska and lovers rock, showing how Jamaican music genres constantly evolved together.\\"},{title:\\"English Beat\\",fact:\\"The English Beat (known as just \'The Beat\' in UK) helped define 2-Tone with songs like \'Mirror in the Bathroom\'.\\"},{title:\\"Reel Big Fish\\",fact:\\"Reel Big Fish brought ska back to mainstream attention in the 90s with \'Sell Out\' - a song about selling out to ska!\\"},{title:\\"Skatellites Return\\",fact:\\"The Skatalites reformed in the 1980s and continued touring well into the 2000s, introducing new generations to original ska.\\"},{title:\\"Save Ferris\\",fact:\\"Save Ferris, named after Ferris Bueller, combined ska with female-fronted pop-punk in the late 90s.\\"},{title:\\"Streetlight Manifesto\\",fact:\\"Streetlight Manifesto pushed ska into progressive territory with complex arrangements and socially conscious lyrics.\\"},{title:\\"Tokyo Ska Paradise\\",fact:\\"Tokyo Ska Paradise Orchestra shows ska\'s global reach - they\'ve been playing Japanese ska since 1988!\\"},{title:\\"Ska Against Racism\\",fact:\\"Ska has long been associated with anti-racism. The 2-Tone movement explicitly promoted racial unity.\\"},{title:\\"Walking Bass\\",fact:\\"The walking bass line in ska provides the foundation for the offbeat guitar, creating that distinctive groove.\\"}],jg=[{title:\\"Neural Networks\\",fact:\\"Neural networks were inspired by biological neurons in the brain. The first artificial neuron, the perceptron, was created in 1958.\\"},{title:\\"AI Agents\\",fact:\\"AI agents are systems that perceive their environment and take actions to maximize their chance of achieving their goals.\\"},{title:\\"Transformer Architecture\\",fact:\\"The Transformer architecture, introduced in 2017, revolutionized NLP. It\'s the \'T\' in GPT and powers modern language models.\\"},{title:\\"Attention Mechanism\\",fact:\\"The attention mechanism allows AI models to focus on relevant parts of input, similar to how humans focus on important details.\\"},{title:\\"Ensemble Methods\\",fact:\\"Ensemble methods combine multiple models to get better predictions - like having a committee of experts vote on decisions.\\"},{title:\\"Prompt Engineering\\",fact:\\"Prompt engineering is the art of crafting effective instructions for AI models. Small changes in prompts can dramatically change outputs.\\"},{title:\\"Fine-tuning\\",fact:\\"Fine-tuning adapts a pre-trained model to specific tasks, like teaching an expert a new specialty within their field.\\"},{title:\\"Tokens\\",fact:\\"Language models process text in \'tokens\' - which can be words, subwords, or characters. \'Ensemble\' might be 2-3 tokens!\\"},{title:\\"Context Window\\",fact:\\"A model\'s context window is how much text it can consider at once. Larger windows mean more understanding of the conversation.\\"},{title:\\"Emergent Abilities\\",fact:\\"As language models get larger, they sometimes develop emergent abilities - skills that weren\'t explicitly programmed.\\"},{title:\\"RLHF\\",fact:\\"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) helps train AI to be more helpful and aligned with human preferences.\\"},{title:\\"Hallucinations\\",fact:\\"AI \'hallucinations\' occur when models confidently generate false information. Good systems have safeguards against this.\\"},{title:\\"Chain of Thought\\",fact:\\"Chain-of-thought prompting encourages AI to show its reasoning steps, leading to better answers on complex problems.\\"},{title:\\"Temperature\\",fact:\\"In AI models, \'temperature\' controls randomness. Low temperature gives focused answers, high temperature gives creative ones.\\"},{title:\\"Zero-Shot Learning\\",fact:\\"Zero-shot learning means an AI can perform tasks it wasn\'t explicitly trained for, using only instructions.\\"},{title:\\"Few-Shot Learning\\",fact:\\"Few-shot learning lets AI learn from just a few examples, like learning to classify after seeing only 5 samples.\\"},{title:\\"Transfer Learning\\",fact:\\"Transfer learning reuses knowledge from one task for another. It\'s why pre-trained models are so powerful.\\"},{title:\\"Tokenization\\",fact:\\"Different tokenizers split text differently. \'Hello world!\' might be 2-4 tokens depending on the model.\\"},{title:\\"Embeddings\\",fact:\\"Word embeddings represent words as vectors in multi-dimensional space. Similar words are close together!\\"},{title:\\"Cosine Similarity\\",fact:\\"Cosine similarity measures how similar two vectors are - it\'s how AI finds relevant documents or similar sentences.\\"},{title:\\"Gradient Descent\\",fact:\\"Gradient descent is how neural networks learn - they follow the \'slope\' to find the best parameters, like rolling downhill.\\"},{title:\\"Backpropagation\\",fact:\\"Backpropagation is how neural networks figure out which weights to adjust - errors flow backward through the network.\\"},{title:\\"Overfitting\\",fact:\\"Overfitting is when a model memorizes training data instead of learning patterns - like memorizing answers instead of understanding.\\"},{title:\\"Regularization\\",fact:\\"Regularization techniques prevent overfitting by adding constraints - like telling a model to keep things simple.\\"},{title:\\"Dropout\\",fact:\\"Dropout randomly \'turns off\' neurons during training, forcing the network to be more robust.\\"},{title:\\"Batch Normalization\\",fact:\\"Batch normalization standardizes inputs to each layer, making training faster and more stable.\\"},{title:\\"Learning Rate\\",fact:\\"The learning rate controls how big of steps the model takes during learning. Too big and it overshoots, too small and it\'s slow.\\"},{title:\\"Loss Function\\",fact:\\"A loss function measures how wrong the model\'s predictions are. Training minimizes this loss.\\"},{title:\\"Activation Functions\\",fact:\\"Activation functions like ReLU introduce non-linearity, allowing neural networks to learn complex patterns.\\"},{title:\\"Convolutional Networks\\",fact:\\"CNNs use sliding filters to detect patterns in images - they can recognize faces, objects, and even code syntax!\\"},{title:\\"Recurrent Networks\\",fact:\\"RNNs process sequences by maintaining memory of previous inputs - useful for language and time series.\\"},{title:\\"LSTM\\",fact:\\"Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks solve RNN\'s memory problems with special \'gates\' that control information flow.\\"},{title:\\"GPT Family\\",fact:\\"GPT stands for \'Generative Pre-trained Transformer\'. Each generation has been dramatically more capable.\\"},{title:\\"Claude\'s Traits\\",fact:\\"Claude is designed to be helpful, harmless, and honest - a framework called Constitutional AI.\\"},{title:\\"Multimodal AI\\",fact:\\"Multimodal AI can understand multiple types of input - text, images, audio, and more - simultaneously.\\"},{title:\\"AI Safety\\",fact:\\"AI safety research ensures AI systems behave as intended and don\'t cause unintended harm.\\"},{title:\\"Benchmark Tests\\",fact:\\"AI models are evaluated on benchmarks like MMLU, HumanEval, and GSM8K to measure their capabilities.\\"},{title:\\"Scaling Laws\\",fact:\\"Scaling laws predict how AI performance improves with more compute, data, and parameters.\\"},{title:\\"Inference vs Training\\",fact:\\"Training is when AI learns, inference is when it makes predictions. Training is much more expensive.\\"},{title:\\"Model Distillation\\",fact:\\"Model distillation creates smaller models that mimic larger ones - getting 80% of the capability at 10% of the cost.\\"}],Ag=[{title:\\"Red-Green-Refactor\\",fact:\\"The TDD cycle is Red (write failing test), Green (make it pass), Refactor (improve code). Repeat!\\"},{title:\\"Test First\\",fact:\\"Writing tests before code forces you to think about design and requirements upfront.\\"},{title:\\"Kent Beck\\",fact:\\"Kent Beck popularized TDD as part of Extreme Programming in the late 1990s.\\"},{title:\\"Confidence to Refactor\\",fact:\\"A comprehensive test suite gives you confidence to refactor without fear of breaking things.\\"},{title:\\"Living Documentation\\",fact:\\"Tests serve as living documentation - they show exactly how code should behave.\\"},{title:\\"Regression Testing\\",fact:\\"Tests catch regressions - when old bugs sneak back in after code changes.\\"},{title:\\"Unit vs Integration\\",fact:\\"Unit tests check individual components, integration tests check how they work together.\\"},{title:\\"Test Coverage\\",fact:\\"Code coverage measures what percentage of code is exercised by tests. 80%+ is often a good target.\\"},{title:\\"Mocking\\",fact:\\"Mocks replace real dependencies in tests, letting you test components in isolation.\\"},{title:\\"Test Fixtures\\",fact:\\"Test fixtures are pre-set data or state used to run tests consistently.\\"},{title:\\"AAA Pattern\\",fact:\\"Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) structures tests: set up data, run code, check results.\\"},{title:\\"Edge Cases\\",fact:\\"Good tests cover edge cases - empty lists, null values, boundary conditions.\\"},{title:\\"Flaky Tests\\",fact:\\"Flaky tests sometimes pass and sometimes fail unpredictably - they undermine trust in the suite.\\"},{title:\\"Test Isolation\\",fact:\\"Tests should be independent - running one test shouldn\'t affect another.\\"},{title:\\"Mutation Testing\\",fact:\\"Mutation testing changes code and checks if tests catch it - finding weak spots in your test suite.\\"},{title:\\"Property-Based Testing\\",fact:\\"Property-based testing generates random inputs to find edge cases you wouldn\'t think of.\\"},{title:\\"BDD\\",fact:\\"Behavior-Driven Development extends TDD by writing tests in natural language (Given-When-Then).\\"},{title:\\"Test Pyramid\\",fact:\\"The test pyramid suggests many unit tests, fewer integration tests, and even fewer E2E tests.\\"},{title:\\"E2E Testing\\",fact:\\"End-to-end tests simulate real user interactions, testing the entire application flow.\\"},{title:\\"Test Doubles\\",fact:\\"Test doubles include mocks, stubs, spies, and fakes - different ways to replace real objects.\\"},{title:\\"Test Speed\\",fact:\\"Fast tests encourage developers to run them often. Slow tests get ignored.\\"},{title:\\"Continuous Testing\\",fact:\\"Running tests automatically on every commit catches problems immediately.\\"},{title:\\"Code Review\\",fact:\\"Reviewing tests during code review ensures quality coverage, not just code.\\"},{title:\\"Test-Driven Design\\",fact:\\"TDD improves design because hard-to-test code often has design issues.\\"},{title:\\"Debugging with Tests\\",fact:\\"When fixing a bug, first write a test that reproduces it, then fix - it won\'t come back.\\"},{title:\\"Parameterized Tests\\",fact:\\"Parameterized tests run the same test with different inputs, reducing duplication.\\"},{title:\\"Snapshot Testing\\",fact:\\"Snapshot tests capture component output and flag changes - useful for UI testing.\\"},{title:\\"Test Organization\\",fact:\\"Organize tests to mirror source code structure - makes finding relevant tests easy.\\"},{title:\\"Assertion Libraries\\",fact:\\"Libraries like Jest, pytest, and JUnit provide expressive ways to write assertions.\\"},{title:\\"Test Reports\\",fact:\\"Test reports show pass/fail status, coverage, and timing - essential for CI/CD.\\"},{title:\\"Test Maintenance\\",fact:\\"Tests need maintenance too! Refactor tests when code changes to keep them valuable.\\"},{title:\\"Contract Testing\\",fact:\\"Contract tests verify that services communicate correctly based on agreed contracts.\\"},{title:\\"Load Testing\\",fact:\\"Load tests verify system behavior under high traffic - essential before launch.\\"},{title:\\"Chaos Engineering\\",fact:\\"Chaos engineering deliberately breaks things to test resilience - Netflix\'s Chaos Monkey is famous.\\"},{title:\\"Visual Regression\\",fact:\\"Visual regression testing catches unintended UI changes by comparing screenshots.\\"},{title:\\"Accessibility Testing\\",fact:\\"Automated accessibility tests check for screen reader compatibility and color contrast.\\"},{title:\\"Security Testing\\",fact:\\"Security tests check for vulnerabilities like SQL injection and XSS.\\"},{title:\\"Performance Testing\\",fact:\\"Performance tests measure response times and resource usage under various conditions.\\"},{title:\\"A/B Testing\\",fact:\\"A/B testing compares two versions to see which performs better with real users.\\"},{title:\\"Canary Releases\\",fact:\\"Canary releases deploy to a small subset of users first, monitoring for issues before full rollout.\\"}],Cg=[{title:\\"Agile Manifesto\\",fact:\\"The Agile Manifesto was written in 2001 by 17 software developers, revolutionizing how we build software.\\"},{title:\\"Technical Debt\\",fact:\\"Technical debt is the cost of shortcuts taken now that must be paid later with interest.\\"},{title:\\"DRY Principle\\",fact:\\"Don\'t Repeat Yourself (DRY) - every piece of knowledge should have a single, unambiguous source.\\"},{title:\\"SOLID Principles\\",fact:\\"SOLID is five design principles: Single responsibility, Open-closed, Liskov substitution, Interface segregation, Dependency inversion.\\"},{title:\\"YAGNI\\",fact:\\"You Aren\'t Gonna Need It (YAGNI) - don\'t build features until they\'re actually needed.\\"},{title:\\"KISS\\",fact:\\"Keep It Simple, Stupid (KISS) - simplicity should be a key goal in design.\\"},{title:\\"Rubber Duck Debugging\\",fact:\\"Rubber duck debugging works by explaining code to a rubber duck - often the solution becomes obvious!\\"},{title:\\"10x Developer\\",fact:\\"The \'10x developer\' myth is debunked - great teams outperform lone geniuses every time.\\"},{title:\\"Code Review\\",fact:\\"Code reviews catch bugs, share knowledge, and improve code quality - they\'re not about criticism.\\"},{title:\\"Pair Programming\\",fact:\\"Pair programming has two developers at one keyboard - it reduces bugs and spreads knowledge.\\"},{title:\\"Mob Programming\\",fact:\\"Mob programming has the whole team at one screen - great for complex problems and learning.\\"},{title:\\"Continuous Integration\\",fact:\\"CI means merging code changes frequently and running automated tests on each merge.\\"},{title:\\"Continuous Deployment\\",fact:\\"CD automatically deploys code that passes all tests - some companies deploy hundreds of times per day.\\"},{title:\\"Feature Flags\\",fact:\\"Feature flags let you deploy code without releasing features - toggle them on when ready.\\"},{title:\\"Blue-Green Deployment\\",fact:\\"Blue-green deployment maintains two production environments, switching traffic for zero-downtime releases.\\"},{title:\\"Microservices\\",fact:\\"Microservices architecture splits applications into small, independent services that can be deployed separately.\\"},{title:\\"Monolith First\\",fact:\\"Many experts recommend starting with a monolith and splitting into microservices only when needed.\\"},{title:\\"API Design\\",fact:\\"Good API design follows REST principles or uses GraphQL for flexible queries.\\"},{title:\\"Rate Limiting\\",fact:\\"Rate limiting prevents abuse by restricting how often users can make requests.\\"},{title:\\"Caching\\",fact:\\"Caching stores frequently accessed data closer to users - it\'s why the web feels fast.\\"},{title:\\"Database Indexing\\",fact:\\"Database indexes speed up queries dramatically - but too many slow down writes.\\"},{title:\\"N+1 Problem\\",fact:\\"The N+1 query problem happens when code makes one query for a list, then N queries for details.\\"},{title:\\"SQL Injection\\",fact:\\"SQL injection is a critical vulnerability where attackers insert malicious SQL into inputs.\\"},{title:\\"XSS Prevention\\",fact:\\"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is prevented by escaping user input before displaying it.\\"},{title:\\"CORS\\",fact:\\"Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) controls which websites can access your API.\\"},{title:\\"JWT Tokens\\",fact:\\"JSON Web Tokens (JWT) are a secure way to transmit information between parties.\\"},{title:\\"OAuth 2.0\\",fact:\\"OAuth 2.0 lets users grant limited access to their accounts without sharing passwords.\\"},{title:\\"Git Flow\\",fact:\\"Git Flow is a branching model with feature, develop, release, and hotfix branches.\\"},{title:\\"Trunk-Based Dev\\",fact:\\"Trunk-based development has everyone commit to main branch frequently - simpler than Git Flow.\\"},{title:\\"Semantic Versioning\\",fact:\\"SemVer uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH - MAJOR for breaking changes, MINOR for features, PATCH for fixes.\\"},{title:\\"Documentation\\",fact:\\"Good documentation is crucial - \'Code tells you how, comments tell you why.\'\\"},{title:\\"README Files\\",fact:\\"A good README helps users and contributors understand and use your project.\\"},{title:\\"Open Source\\",fact:\\"Open source software powers most of the internet - from Linux to React to Python.\\"},{title:\\"Tech Stack\\",fact:\\"Your tech stack is the combination of technologies used to build your application.\\"},{title:\\"DevOps Culture\\",fact:\\"DevOps culture emphasizes collaboration between development and operations teams.\\"},{title:\\"Infrastructure as Code\\",fact:\\"IaC treats infrastructure configuration like application code - versioned and reproducible.\\"},{title:\\"Containers\\",fact:\\"Containers package applications with their dependencies for consistent deployment anywhere.\\"},{title:\\"Kubernetes\\",fact:\\"Kubernetes orchestrates containers at scale, handling deployment, scaling, and management.\\"},{title:\\"Serverless\\",fact:\\"Serverless computing runs code without managing servers - you just write functions.\\"},{title:\\"Edge Computing\\",fact:\\"Edge computing processes data closer to users, reducing latency for real-time applications.\\"}],Og=[{title:\\"Big O Notation\\",fact:\\"Big O notation describes algorithm efficiency - O(n) is linear, O(n\\u00b2) is quadratic. Lower is usually better!\\"},{title:\\"Premature Optimization\\",fact:\\"Premature optimization is the root of all evil - but knowing when to optimize is wisdom.\\"},{title:\\"Latency Numbers\\",fact:\\"L1 cache: 0.5ns, RAM: 100ns, SSD: 150\\u00b5s, Network: 150ms. Know your latency numbers!\\"},{title:\\"Caching Strategies\\",fact:\\"Cache invalidation is one of the two hard problems in CS (the other is naming things).\\"},{title:\\"Database Queries\\",fact:\\"One slow database query can cripple your entire application. Profile and optimize!\\"},{title:\\"Lazy Loading\\",fact:\\"Lazy loading defers loading resources until needed, speeding up initial page load.\\"},{title:\\"CDN Benefits\\",fact:\\"Content Delivery Networks cache content globally, reducing latency for users worldwide.\\"},{title:\\"Compression\\",fact:\\"GZIP compression can reduce file sizes by 70%+ - huge impact on load times.\\"},{title:\\"Image Optimization\\",fact:\\"Optimized images can be 80% smaller without visible quality loss. Use WebP format!\\"},{title:\\"Bundle Splitting\\",fact:\\"Code splitting loads only necessary JavaScript, reducing initial bundle size.\\"},{title:\\"Tree Shaking\\",fact:\\"Tree shaking removes unused code from bundles - like shaking a tree to drop dead leaves.\\"},{title:\\"Virtual DOM\\",fact:\\"React\'s Virtual DOM minimizes expensive DOM operations by batching updates.\\"},{title:\\"Memory Leaks\\",fact:\\"Memory leaks accumulate over time, eventually crashing applications or slowing them down.\\"},{title:\\"Garbage Collection\\",fact:\\"Garbage collection automatically frees unused memory, but has performance overhead.\\"},{title:\\"Connection Pooling\\",fact:\\"Database connection pooling reuses connections instead of creating new ones - much faster.\\"},{title:\\"Asynchronous IO\\",fact:\\"Async IO lets a single thread handle thousands of connections efficiently.\\"},{title:\\"Load Balancing\\",fact:\\"Load balancers distribute traffic across servers, improving reliability and performance.\\"},{title:\\"Horizontal Scaling\\",fact:\\"Horizontal scaling adds more machines; vertical scaling makes machines bigger.\\"},{title:\\"Auto-scaling\\",fact:\\"Auto-scaling automatically adjusts resources based on demand, saving cost and handling spikes.\\"},{title:\\"Response Times\\",fact:\\"Users notice delays above 100ms. Above 1 second, they start getting frustrated.\\"},{title:\\"First Contentful Paint\\",fact:\\"FCP measures when content first appears - aim for under 1.8 seconds.\\"},{title:\\"Time to Interactive\\",fact:\\"TTI measures when the page becomes fully interactive - crucial for user experience.\\"},{title:\\"Core Web Vitals\\",fact:\\"Google\'s Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS) affect search rankings and user experience.\\"},{title:\\"HTTP/2\\",fact:\\"HTTP/2 multiplexes requests over a single connection, dramatically speeding up page loads.\\"},{title:\\"Service Workers\\",fact:\\"Service workers enable offline functionality and faster repeat visits through caching.\\"},{title:\\"Web Workers\\",fact:\\"Web Workers run JavaScript in background threads, keeping the UI responsive.\\"},{title:\\"Debouncing\\",fact:\\"Debouncing limits how often a function runs - essential for search inputs and resize handlers.\\"},{title:\\"Throttling\\",fact:\\"Throttling ensures a function runs at most once per time period - useful for scroll handlers.\\"},{title:\\"Batch Processing\\",fact:\\"Batch processing groups operations together, reducing overhead from individual calls.\\"},{title:\\"Pagination\\",fact:\\"Pagination loads data in chunks instead of all at once - essential for large datasets.\\"},{title:\\"Infinite Scroll\\",fact:\\"Infinite scroll loads more content as you scroll, but can have accessibility issues.\\"},{title:\\"Request Coalescing\\",fact:\\"Request coalescing combines duplicate requests into one, reducing server load.\\"},{title:\\"Query Optimization\\",fact:\\"EXPLAIN ANALYZE reveals how databases execute queries, helping optimization.\\"},{title:\\"Denormalization\\",fact:\\"Denormalization trades storage for speed by duplicating data to avoid joins.\\"},{title:\\"Read Replicas\\",fact:\\"Read replicas distribute read queries across multiple database servers.\\"},{title:\\"In-Memory Databases\\",fact:\\"Redis and Memcached store data in RAM for sub-millisecond access times.\\"},{title:\\"Profiling Tools\\",fact:\\"Profiling tools like Chrome DevTools and py-spy reveal where time is actually spent.\\"},{title:\\"Flame Graphs\\",fact:\\"Flame graphs visualize call stacks, making it easy to spot performance bottlenecks.\\"},{title:\\"Amdahl\'s Law\\",fact:\\"Amdahl\'s Law shows that speedup is limited by the serial portion of a task.\\"},{title:\\"P99 Latency\\",fact:\\"P99 latency is the 99th percentile - more useful than averages for real user experience.\\"}],zg=[{title:\\"The Nines\\",fact:\\"99.9% uptime means 8.76 hours of downtime per year. 99.99% means just 52 minutes.\\"},{title:\\"SLOs and SLAs\\",fact:\\"SLOs are internal targets, SLAs are customer promises. Don\'t make them identical!\\"},{title:\\"Error Budgets\\",fact:\\"Error budgets define how much unreliability is acceptable - when exhausted, focus on stability.\\"},{title:\\"Circuit Breakers\\",fact:\\"Circuit breakers prevent cascading failures by stopping calls to failing services.\\"},{title:\\"Retry Logic\\",fact:\\"Smart retry logic with exponential backoff handles transient failures gracefully.\\"},{title:\\"Timeout Handling\\",fact:\\"Timeouts prevent requests from waiting forever - always set appropriate timeouts.\\"},{title:\\"Graceful Degradation\\",fact:\\"Graceful degradation keeps core functionality working when dependencies fail.\\"},{title:\\"Fallback Strategies\\",fact:\\"Fallbacks provide alternatives when primary systems fail - cached data, default values, etc.\\"},{title:\\"Health Checks\\",fact:\\"Health checks let load balancers route traffic away from unhealthy instances.\\"},{title:\\"Heartbeats\\",fact:\\"Heartbeats detect whether services are alive and responsive.\\"},{title:\\"Watchdog Timers\\",fact:\\"Watchdog timers restart processes that become unresponsive.\\"},{title:\\"Chaos Engineering\\",fact:\\"Deliberately breaking things in production finds weaknesses before they cause outages.\\"},{title:\\"Game Days\\",fact:\\"Game days simulate incidents so teams practice their response before real emergencies.\\"},{title:\\"Incident Response\\",fact:\\"Good incident response requires clear roles: incident commander, communicator, experts.\\"},{title:\\"Blameless Postmortems\\",fact:\\"Blameless postmortems focus on system improvements, not individual blame.\\"},{title:\\"Root Cause Analysis\\",fact:\\"5 Whys and fishbone diagrams help find the true root cause of incidents.\\"},{title:\\"Observability\\",fact:\\"Observability is about understanding system state from external outputs - logs, metrics, traces.\\"},{title:\\"Logging Best Practices\\",fact:\\"Structured logging with correlation IDs makes debugging distributed systems possible.\\"},{title:\\"Distributed Tracing\\",fact:\\"Distributed tracing follows requests across services, revealing performance bottlenecks.\\"},{title:\\"Alerting Philosophy\\",fact:\\"Alert on symptoms (user-facing issues), not causes. Reduce noise to prevent fatigue.\\"},{title:\\"On-Call Rotations\\",fact:\\"Fair on-call rotations prevent burnout and ensure coverage around the clock.\\"},{title:\\"Runbooks\\",fact:\\"Runbooks document how to handle common incidents - essential for on-call engineers.\\"},{title:\\"Disaster Recovery\\",fact:\\"DR plans define how to recover from major outages - and they need regular testing.\\"},{title:\\"RTO and RPO\\",fact:\\"Recovery Time Objective (how fast) and Recovery Point Objective (how much data loss) define DR requirements.\\"},{title:\\"Backup Verification\\",fact:\\"Backups are worthless if they can\'t be restored. Test your backups regularly!\\"},{title:\\"Data Replication\\",fact:\\"Synchronous replication guarantees consistency; asynchronous improves performance.\\"},{title:\\"Consensus Protocols\\",fact:\\"Raft and Paxos protocols help distributed systems agree even when some nodes fail.\\"},{title:\\"Split-Brain Problem\\",fact:\\"Network partitions can cause split-brain where parts of a system disagree.\\"},{title:\\"CAP Theorem\\",fact:\\"CAP theorem: you can only have 2 of 3 - Consistency, Availability, Partition tolerance.\\"},{title:\\"Eventual Consistency\\",fact:\\"Eventually consistent systems prioritize availability and sync data later.\\"},{title:\\"Idempotency\\",fact:\\"Idempotent operations produce the same result no matter how many times they run.\\"},{title:\\"Message Queues\\",fact:\\"Message queues decouple services and handle spikes by buffering requests.\\"},{title:\\"Dead Letter Queues\\",fact:\\"Dead letter queues capture failed messages for later analysis and reprocessing.\\"},{title:\\"Rate Limiting\\",fact:\\"Rate limiting protects services from being overwhelmed by too many requests.\\"},{title:\\"Bulkheads\\",fact:\\"Bulkhead pattern isolates components so failures don\'t spread system-wide.\\"},{title:\\"Immutable Infrastructure\\",fact:\\"Immutable infrastructure replaces servers instead of updating them - more reliable.\\"},{title:\\"Blue-Green Deployments\\",fact:\\"Blue-green keeps two production environments, enabling instant rollback.\\"},{title:\\"Canary Releases\\",fact:\\"Canary releases test changes on a small percentage of users before full rollout.\\"},{title:\\"Rolling Updates\\",fact:\\"Rolling updates gradually replace instances, maintaining capacity throughout.\\"},{title:\\"Feature Flags\\",fact:\\"Feature flags let you disable features instantly if problems occur in production.\\"}],_g=[{title:\\"Code Lines\\",fact:\\"The average developer writes about 50 lines of production code per day - quality takes time!\\"},{title:\\"Bug Ratio\\",fact:\\"Industry average is about 15-50 bugs per 1000 lines of code. That\'s why we test!\\"},{title:\\"Meeting Cost\\",fact:\\"A 1-hour meeting with 10 engineers costs the company $500-$1500 in salaries.\\"},{title:\\"Context Switching\\",fact:\\"It takes 23 minutes on average to regain focus after an interruption.\\"},{title:\\"Code Reviews\\",fact:\\"Code reviews catch 60% of defects on average - more than any other technique.\\"},{title:\\"Documentation Time\\",fact:\\"Engineers spend about 30% of their time reading and understanding code.\\"},{title:\\"Technical Debt\\",fact:\\"Companies spend up to 40% of engineering time dealing with technical debt.\\"},{title:\\"Open Source Stats\\",fact:\\"GitHub hosts over 200 million repositories with 100 million developers.\\"},{title:\\"Programming Languages\\",fact:\\"There are over 700 programming languages in active use today.\\"},{title:\\"JavaScript Usage\\",fact:\\"JavaScript is used by 97.8% of all websites as of 2024.\\"},{title:\\"Python Growth\\",fact:\\"Python became the most popular programming language in 2021, passing Java and C.\\"},{title:\\"Stack Overflow\\",fact:\\"Stack Overflow has over 23 million questions and 34 million answers.\\"},{title:\\"npm Packages\\",fact:\\"npm has over 2 million packages - the largest package registry in the world.\\"},{title:\\"Lines in Linux\\",fact:\\"The Linux kernel has over 30 million lines of code, written by 15,000+ developers.\\"},{title:\\"Chrome Codebase\\",fact:\\"Google Chrome has about 35 million lines of code and took years to develop.\\"},{title:\\"AWS Services\\",fact:\\"AWS has over 200 services - no one knows them all!\\"},{title:\\"Internet Traffic\\",fact:\\"About 5 exabytes of data flow through the internet daily. That\'s 5 billion gigabytes!\\"},{title:\\"API Calls\\",fact:\\"Stripe processes hundreds of millions of API calls per day.\\"},{title:\\"Deployment Frequency\\",fact:\\"Top-performing teams deploy multiple times per day; struggling teams deploy monthly.\\"},{title:\\"MTTR\\",fact:\\"Elite teams recover from failures in under an hour. Low performers take over a week.\\"},{title:\\"Change Failure Rate\\",fact:\\"High-performing teams have change failure rates under 15%.\\"},{title:\\"Lead Time\\",fact:\\"Elite teams go from commit to deploy in under an hour.\\"},{title:\\"Pair Programming Study\\",fact:\\"Studies show 